      "text": "To find the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to determine the area of the two sectors of the circle and then subtract the area of the triangle formed by the two radii and the arc between the two sectors.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one sector. The radius of the circle is 12, and each sector has a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). The area \\(A_{\\text{sector}}\\) of a sector of a circle with radius \\(r\\) and central angle \\(\\theta\\) (in degrees) is given by:\n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{sector}} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\n\nFor our sector, \\(r = 12\\) and \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{sector}} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\n\nSince there are two such sectors, the total area of the two sectors is:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the triangle \\(ABD\\). The triangle \\(ABD\\) is isosceles with two sides of length 12 and an included angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). This means that triangle \\(ABD\\) is an equilateral triangle. The area \\(A_{\\text{triangle}}\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{triangle}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\n\nFor our triangle, \\(s = 12\\):\n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{triangle}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 12^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 144 = 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is the total area of the two sectors minus the area of the triangle:\n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{ABCD}} = 48\\pi - 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi - 36\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
