      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the area of figure \\(ABCD\\), we start by analyzing the given information and the geometric configuration of the sectors.\n\n1. **Identify the shape and properties:**\n   - We have two sectors of a circle, each with a radius of 12.\n   - Each sector has a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the area of one sector:**\n   - The formula for the area of a sector of a circle is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n     \\]\n   - Here, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(r = 12\\).\n   - Substituting the values, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 24\\pi\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the area of figure \\(ABCD\\):**\n   - Figure \\(ABCD\\) consists of two sectors placed side by side.\n   - Since there are two sectors, the total area is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total area} = 2 \\times \\text{Area of one sector}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 2 \\times 24\\pi\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 48\\pi\n     \\]\n\n4. **Final answer:**\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{48\\pi}\n   \\]",
