      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we start by analyzing the given information and the geometry of the problem. We have two sectors of a circle with a radius of 12, each with a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). The sectors are placed side by side, forming a smaller equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\) with side length 12.\n\nFirst, we calculate the area of one sector. The formula for the area of a sector of a circle is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius of the circle. For our sectors, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(r = 12\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince there are two such sectors, the total area of the two sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of two sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\nNext, we need to find the area of the equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\). The side length of the equilateral triangle is 12. The formula for the area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of an equilateral triangle} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(s = 12\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of triangle } ABC = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 12^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 144 = 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThe area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is the sum of the area of the two sectors and the area of the triangle \\(ABC\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure } ABCD = 48\\pi + 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi + 36\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
